When a suite gets big enough that one process is the bottleneck, Curlew shards the
main phase across a fleet of workers. And when you need to know how an endpoint holds
up under pressure, the `perf` command turns one request file into a load test.

## Distributed runs

The coordinator hands shards of the main phase to workers; setup and teardown still
run exactly once, locally. Extracted setup variables propagate to every worker.

```bash
export CURLEW_COORDINATOR_URL=https://coordinator.example.com
export CURLEW_BACKEND_TOKEN=<bearer>

curlew run 'collections/**/*.yaml' \
  --workers 10 \
  --org acme
```

On each worker host:

```bash
curlew worker --job <job-id>
```

`--workers 1` falls back to plain local execution — a clean way to gate on pipeline
capacity. Unauthorized workers exit with code `10`.

## Load testing a hot path

```bash
curlew perf requests/hot-path.yaml \
  --vus 50 \
  --duration 2m \
  --ramp-up 30s \
  --rps 500 \
  --output report.html
```

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--vus <n>` | Virtual users (concurrent workers) |
| `--duration <d>` | Total run duration (`30s`, `2m`, `1h`) |
| `--ramp-up <d>` | Linearly ramp 1→N VUs over this window |
| `--rps <n>` | Target requests/sec (`0` = let the VUs sprint) |
| `--output` | Destination — `stdout`, or a `.json` / `.html` report |

```
curlew perf — hot-path.yaml
  VUs: 50  duration: 2m  target: 500 rps

  requests   58,420      errors  0 (0.00%)
  throughput 487.1 rps
  latency    p50 38ms   p95 102ms   p99 184ms   max 421ms

Ctrl+C writes the partial summary and exits 130.
```

<Callout type="output">
	Representative metrics — actual numbers depend on the target.
</Callout>

<Callout type="warn">
	Load testing generates real traffic and can run up a real bill. Only point
	<code>perf</code> at endpoints you own or are explicitly authorized to test —
	never a third party's production API.
</Callout>
